Step 12 – Create the AgentCore Runtime IAM role
The agent needs its own IAM role because it must:
1. Invoke the existing StudentLookupTool Lambda.
2. Call the Claude model through Amazon Bedrock.
3. Write application logs to CloudWatch.
This is a separate role from StudentLookupLambdaRole. We will create the new role first.
12.1 Create the trust policy
The trust policy tells AWS that Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime is allowed to assume this IAM role.

Run the following commands in your terminal (macOS/Linux or Windows Git Bash):
cd ~/student-agent-demo/deployment

export AWS_REGION=ap-south-1

ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)

cat <<'EOF' > agentcore-trust-policy.json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowAgentCoreRuntime",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Service": "bedrock-agentcore.amazonaws.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:SourceAccount": "ACCOUNT_ID_PLACEHOLDER"
        },
        "ArnLike": {
          "aws:SourceArn": "arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:ap-south-1:ACCOUNT_ID_PLACEHOLDER:*"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
EOF

sed "s/ACCOUNT_ID_PLACEHOLDER/$ACCOUNT_ID/g" agentcore-trust-policy.json > agentcore-trust-policy-final.json


12.2 Create the IAM role
aws iam create-role \
  --role-name StudentAgentCoreExecutionRole \
  --assume-role-policy-document file://agentcore-trust-policy-final.json \
  --description "Execution role for Student Agent on AgentCore Runtime"


12.3 Verify
